import mundane theorem    stdlib/geometry/between-by-coordinates
import mundane theorem    stdlib/geometry/between-collinear

theorem three-points-on-a-line
  note        By the coordinates, B is between A and C: the vectors from B
              to A and to C are parallel, their cross product being 0, and
              point opposite ways. A point between two others is on their
              line.
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume A = (1, 1)                                                   (H4)
  assume B = (2, 3)                                                   (H5)
  assume C = (4, 7)                                                   (H6)
  then A, B, C are collinear. B is between A and C.

1.  (1 − 2)·(7 − 3) = (4 − 2)·(1 − 3)
    arithmetic

2.  (1 − 2)·(4 − 2) + (1 − 3)·(7 − 3) < 0
    arithmetic

3.  (2, 3) is between (1, 1) and (4, 7)
    mun:between-by-coordinates, from 1, 2

4.  B is between (1, 1) and (4, 7)
    substitute B = (2, 3) (H5) into line 3

5.  B is between A and (4, 7)
    substitute A = (1, 1) (H4) into line 4

6.  B is between A and C
    substitute C = (4, 7) (H6) into line 5

7.  A, B, C are collinear
    mun:between-collinear, from 6

8.  A, B, C are collinear. B is between A and C.
    join 7, 6
